Persist the MI_AO codec across SDL audio open/close.

WHY
---
The audible pop when a game exits IS the MI_AO analog power-down. This is measured, not inferred:
holding the codec open made the exit pop vanish completely (confirmed by ear on device), while
muting and rail-settling around the disable changed nothing — the data path was never involved.

The stock MMIYOO driver enables the codec on every SDL_OpenAudio and disables it on every
SDL_CloseAudio, so every game launch and every game exit powers the analog stage up and down.

WHY NOT THE MyMinUI APPROACH
----------------------------
MyMinUI (other/sdl2.patch) solves the same problem by deleting the MI_AO calls entirely and
routing audio through /dev/dsp into the `audioserver` daemon, which owns the hardware for the
lifetime of the system ("il demone terrà l'hardware acceso (Zero Pop!)").

That works, but it costs a resident daemon (idle CPU/power — the thing this fork exists to
minimise) plus a socket hop of latency, which is why they then had to write a lower-latency
audioserver replacement. We keep talking to MI_AO directly and simply stop cycling it.

WHAT THIS CHANGES
-----------------
1. CloseDevice no longer disables. There is nothing to power down, so nothing to pop.

2. OpenDevice ADOPTS an already-configured device instead of failing. MI_AO_SetPubAttr cannot
   reconfigure a device that is still enabled — it returns 0xa0052009 — and the stock driver
   treats that as fatal. That is exactly why our first attempt at keeping the codec open left the
   SECOND game silent: the open "succeeded" at the SDL layer only after our own recovery path
   disabled the codec again (re-introducing the pop) or failed outright.

   Every core on this device resolves to the same 48kHz output (MEASURED: gambatte/gpsp/fceumm/
   supafaust/picodrive all report "48000 (rec)"), so an already-configured device is already
   configured the way we want it. The GetPubAttr call is kept as a sanity read rather than a
   gate.

UNVERIFIED: this patch has not yet been built or heard. It must not ship until there is a way to
verify AUDIBILITY automatically — "SDL_OpenAudio returned 0" is not the same claim as "sound came
out", and trusting that difference is what let an audio regression reach the device twice.

--- a/src/audio/mmiyoo/SDL_audio_mmiyoo.c
+++ b/src/audio/mmiyoo/SDL_audio_mmiyoo.c
@@
 static void MMIYOO_CloseDevice(_THIS)
 {
     SDL_free(this->hidden->mixbuf);
     SDL_free(this->hidden);
 #if defined(MMIYOO)
-    MI_AO_DisableChn(AoDevId, AoChn);
-    MI_AO_Disable(AoDevId);
+    /* Deliberately DO NOT disable. MI_AO_Disable powers the analog stage down and that
+       transition is the audible pop on game exit (measured on device). The codec is left
+       enabled for the next process to adopt; MMIYOO_OpenDevice handles that case. */
 #endif
 }
@@
     miret = MI_AO_SetPubAttr(AoDevId, &stSetAttr);
     if(miret != MI_SUCCESS) {
-        printf("%s, failed to set PubAttr\n", __func__);
-        return -1;
+        /* The device is still enabled from a previous process: SetPubAttr cannot reconfigure a
+           live device. Do NOT treat this as fatal and do NOT disable to reconfigure — that
+           power-down is precisely the pop we are removing. Adopt the existing configuration. */
+        printf("%s, PubAttr rejected (device already enabled) — adopting existing config\n", __func__);
+        already_enabled = 1;
     }
-    miret = MI_AO_GetPubAttr(AoDevId, &stGetAttr);
-    if(miret != MI_SUCCESS) {
-        printf("%s, failed to get PubAttr\n", __func__);
-        return -1;
-    }
-    miret = MI_AO_Enable(AoDevId);
-    if(miret != MI_SUCCESS) {
-        printf("%s, failed to enable AO\n", __func__);
-        return -1;
-    }
-    miret = MI_AO_EnableChn(AoDevId, AoChn);
-    if(miret != MI_SUCCESS) {
-        printf("%s, failed to enable Channel\n", __func__);
-        return -1;
-    }
+    /* Sanity read only — never a gate. */
+    MI_AO_GetPubAttr(AoDevId, &stGetAttr);
+
+    if(!already_enabled) {
+        miret = MI_AO_Enable(AoDevId);
+        if(miret != MI_SUCCESS) {
+            printf("%s, failed to enable AO\n", __func__);
+            return -1;
+        }
+    }
+    /* Enabling an already-enabled channel is not an error for us. */
+    miret = MI_AO_EnableChn(AoDevId, AoChn);
+    if(miret != MI_SUCCESS && !already_enabled) {
+        printf("%s, failed to enable Channel\n", __func__);
+        return -1;
+    }
